QUESTIONS = [
    # CH49 UPSC & Service Commissions L1 (+27)
    {"text":"The Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) is established under which Article of the Constitution?","options":["Article 315","Article 316","Article 317","Article 320"],"correct_answer":"Article 315","explanation":"Article 315 provides for the establishment of a Public Service Commission for the Union and for each State. The Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) is the central recruitment body for civil services.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Chairman of UPSC is appointed by:","options":["The Prime Minister","The President of India","The Chief Justice of India","A collegium of senior civil servants"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"Article 316 provides that the Chairman and members of UPSC are appointed by the President of India. Similarly, State PSC Chairmen are appointed by the Governor.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"How can the Chairman of UPSC be removed from office?","options":["By the President on grounds of proved misbehaviour after SC inquiry","By Parliamentary address in both Houses","By the Prime Minister's recommendation to President","By majority resolution of the UPSC itself"],"correct_answer":"By the President on grounds of proved misbehaviour after SC inquiry","explanation":"Article 317 provides that the Chairman/member of UPSC can only be removed by the President on grounds of proved misbehaviour after the Supreme Court holds an inquiry and recommends removal — a high constitutional protection.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The UPSC Chairman after retirement is NOT eligible for:","options":["A further appointment as Chairman","Further employment under the Government of India or a State Government","Teaching positions at universities","Membership in state human rights commissions"],"correct_answer":"Further employment under the Government of India or a State Government","explanation":"Article 316(4) provides that a person who holds office as Chairman of the UPSC is not eligible for further employment either under the GoI or a State Government after his/her term. This ensures independence.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The primary functions of UPSC are related to:","options":["Recruitment, promotion, and discipline of All India Service officers","Recruitment to civil posts and advising on matters of personnel policy","Setting pay scales and service rules for central employees","Managing the Indian Foreign Service exclusively"],"correct_answer":"Recruitment to civil posts and advising on matters of personnel policy","explanation":"Article 320 lists UPSC's functions: conducting examinations for civil service appointments, advising on methods of recruitment, principles of promotion, and disciplinary matters. It is fundamentally an advisory and examination body.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The UPSC presents an annual report on its work to:","options":["The Prime Minister","The Parliament directly","The President, who causes it to be laid before Parliament","The Lok Sabha Speaker"],"correct_answer":"The President, who causes it to be laid before Parliament","explanation":"Article 323 requires UPSC to present an annual report to the President, who then causes it (along with a memorandum explaining cases where UPSC advice was not accepted) to be laid before each House of Parliament.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"A State Public Service Commission can be asked to serve the needs of another state or the Union. This is done:","options":["Automatically if both states agree","By the President with the consent of the Governor of the state","By an Act of Parliament or State Legislature","By order of the Supreme Court"],"correct_answer":"By the President with the consent of the Governor of the state","explanation":"Article 315(2) enables the President, in consultation with the Governor, to authorise a State PSC to serve the needs of another state or the Union. The UPSC can also take over state PSC functions under Article 315.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which examination conducted by UPSC selects officers for the Indian Administrative Service (IAS), Indian Police Service (IPS), and Indian Foreign Service (IFS)?","options":["Combined Defence Services Exam","Civil Services Examination (CSE)","Combined Medical Services Exam","Central Armed Police Forces Exam"],"correct_answer":"Civil Services Examination (CSE)","explanation":"The Civil Services Examination (CSE), conducted annually by UPSC, is the main route to joining central services including IAS, IPS, IFS, IRS, and 20+ other services. It is one of the world's largest and most competitive examinations.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"UPSC is NOT required to be consulted on which of the following?","options":["Promotions in Central services involving a change of service","Recruitment for temporary appointments lasting less than a year","Matters relating to suitability of IAS officers for posting","Disciplinary matters affecting Central Government employees"],"correct_answer":"Recruitment for temporary appointments lasting less than a year","explanation":"Article 320(3) requires UPSC consultation on various matters. However, Article 321 allows Parliament/state legislatures to specify matters where UPSC need not be consulted — including temporary posts of under one year.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Chairman or member of UPSC can be suspended from office pending inquiry by:","options":["The Supreme Court","The President","Parliament by resolution","The Chief Vigilance Commissioner"],"correct_answer":"The President","explanation":"Article 317(3) provides that the President may suspend a UPSC Chairman or member pending inquiry by the SC — the President acts to protect public interest while the formal removal inquiry is underway.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The minimum constitutional requirement for the composition of UPSC membership is:","options":["At least 50% must have government service experience","At least 1 member must have retired from the judiciary","No constitutional minimum — it is discretionary by the President","Parliament prescribes the number"],"correct_answer":"No constitutional minimum — it is discretionary by the President","explanation":"Article 316 states that the UPSC shall consist of a Chairman and 'such other members as the President may from time to time fix.' There is no constitutional minimum, but one-half must have Government service experience of at least 10 years.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"A member of UPSC holds office for a term of:","options":["5 years or until age 62, whichever is earlier","6 years or until age 65, whichever is earlier","3 years renewable once","5 years renewable for one term"],"correct_answer":"6 years or until age 65, whichever is earlier","explanation":"Article 316(2): A UPSC member holds office for 6 years OR until they attain the age of 65 years, whichever is earlier. SPSC members serve 6 years or age 62.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The National Recruitment Agency (NRA) proposed to replace which of the following?","options":["UPSC civil services examination","SSC, IBPS, and Railways recruitment board preliminary examinations with a Common Eligibility Test","State PSC examinations","Both UPSC and SSC examinations"],"correct_answer":"SSC, IBPS, and Railways recruitment board preliminary examinations with a Common Eligibility Test","explanation":"NRA was approved in 2020 to replace the separate preliminary exams of SSC (Staff Selection Commission), IBPS (banking), and Ministry of Railways with a single Common Eligibility Test (CET) for non-gazetted posts.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"UPSC has no role in appointments to which of the following?","options":["Indian Administrative Service","Indian Police Service","State civil services under state governments","Indian Foreign Service"],"correct_answer":"State civil services under state governments","explanation":"Article 320 and the overall scheme vest UPSC's mandate in central services. State PSCs handle recruitment for state civil services. UPSC's role for state services is limited — it handles IAS/IPS which are All India Services.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is a Ground for mandatory removal of UPSC Chairman?","options":["Engaging in paid employment outside UPSC duties","Failure to qualify annual performance review","Being away from India for more than 30 days","Not attending more than 3 consecutive UPSC meetings"],"correct_answer":"Engaging in paid employment outside UPSC duties","explanation":"Article 317(1)(b) — a UPSC member MUST be removed if they engage in any paid employment outside the duties of office. This is one of the automatic grounds requiring mandatory removal (along with insolvency, etc.).","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The UPSC can extend its services to specific public sector undertakings. This happens when:","options":["Any PSU requests UPSC assistance on its own","An Act of Parliament specifically designates a body for UPSC service","The President by executive order extends UPSC coverage","The Finance Ministry approves the arrangement"],"correct_answer":"An Act of Parliament specifically designates a body for UPSC service","explanation":"Article 321 empowers Parliament to extend UPSC's functions to states or classes of establishments not covered under Article 315. This requires Parliamentary legislation.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Lateral Entry scheme allows direct induction into central services at Joint Secretary level. This scheme:","options":["Bypasses UPSC completely — recruitment is through DOPT directly","Is conducted with UPSC involvement for shortlisting candidates","Requires constitutional amendment to be valid","Was held unconstitutional by the Supreme Court"],"correct_answer":"Is conducted with UPSC involvement for shortlisting candidates","explanation":"The Lateral Entry scheme (announced 2018) initially bypassed UPSC, but since 2019-2020, UPSC has been involved in the selection process. The scheme has not been held unconstitutional.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Article of the Constitution empowers Parliament to extend the functions of UPSC?","options":["Article 315","Article 318","Article 321","Article 323"],"correct_answer":"Article 321","explanation":"Article 321 specifically empowers Parliament to extend the functions of the UPSC or a State PSC to include additional services and posts not originally covered under the constitutional scheme.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"In which year was the current Civil Services Examination (CSE) format (Preliminary + Mains + Interview) established?","options":["1950","1979","1993","2011"],"correct_answer":"1979","explanation":"The current three-stage CSE format (Preliminary + Mains + Personality Test) was established in 1979 based on the Kothari Commission recommendations (1974). Earlier formats varied.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The UPSC is constitutionally protected from government interference primarily through which provisions?","options":["Fixed tenure and difficult removal procedure for Chairman/Members","Government has no role in UPSC's day-to-day functioning","UPSC is above Parliament's jurisdiction","UPSC has sovereign immunity from all courts"],"correct_answer":"Fixed tenure and difficult removal procedure for Chairman/Members","explanation":"The constitutional protections for UPSC independence are: fixed 6-year tenure, removal only through SC inquiry, ineligibility for further government employment after term, and charge on Consolidated Fund of India — all ensuring insulation from political pressure.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following best describes the difference between UPSC and Staff Selection Commission (SSC)?","options":["UPSC selects Group A and B gazetted posts; SSC selects non-gazetted Group B and C posts","UPSC selects defence services; SSC selects civil services","They are identical bodies serving different ministries","SSC is constitutional; UPSC is statutory"],"correct_answer":"UPSC selects Group A and B gazetted posts; SSC selects non-gazetted Group B and C posts","explanation":"UPSC is the constitutional body for Group A gazetted central services (IAS, IPS, etc.) and Group B gazetted posts. SSC is a statutory body recruiting for Group B non-gazetted and Group C central government posts.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Article makes UPSC's expenses a charge on the Consolidated Fund of India?","options":["Article 266","Article 112","Article 322","Article 315"],"correct_answer":"Article 322","explanation":"Article 322 provides that the salaries, allowances, and pensions of UPSC members shall be charged on the Consolidated Fund of India (not voted in Parliament). This ensures independence — their salaries cannot be reduced by Parliament as a pressure tactic.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The UPSC advises the Government on matters related to:","options":["Framing of recruitment rules for civil services posts","Defence procurement policy","Foreign policy formulation","Setting income tax slabs"],"correct_answer":"Framing of recruitment rules for civil services posts","explanation":"Article 320(3)(a) requires the Government to consult UPSC on the methods of recruitment to civil services and posts. This includes framing service rules, promotion criteria, and transfer policies for gazetted posts.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"A person who was a member of a State Public Service Commission can be appointed to UPSC. This appointment is made by:","options":["The Governor of the state concerned","The President of India","The Prime Minister on cabinet recommendation","The Chief Justice of India"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"Article 316 provides that both the UPSC Chairman/members and State PSC Chairmen/members are appointed by the President and the Governor respectively. A former State PSC member can be elevated to UPSC — the President makes the appointment.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Civil Services Day in India is celebrated annually on which date?","options":["January 26","August 15","April 21","November 20"],"correct_answer":"April 21","explanation":"Civil Services Day is celebrated on April 21 every year — the day Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel made his historic address to the Indian Administrative Service recruits in 1947, calling them the 'steel frame of India.'","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The 'steel frame' metaphor for the Indian civil services was used by:","options":["Jawaharlal Nehru","Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","B.R. Ambedkar","Lord Mountbatten"],"correct_answer":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","explanation":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, as Deputy PM and Home Minister, described the Indian Administrative Service as the 'steel frame of India' in his April 21, 1947 address — emphasising the IAS as the backbone of national administration.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"The UPSC recommendations in civil service matters are:","options":["Absolutely binding on the Government","Advisory — the Government can reject them but must state reasons in the annual report","Binding only on Group A posts","Binding only for initial appointments, not promotions"],"correct_answer":"Advisory — the Government can reject them but must state reasons in the annual report","explanation":"UPSC recommendations are advisory, not binding. However, if not accepted, Article 323 requires the Government to record its reasons in the annual report presented to Parliament — creating a layer of accountability.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Constitutional Article deals with the State Public Service Commission (SPSC)?","options":["Article 315 (same as UPSC)","Article 316 only","Article 315 covers both UPSC and SPSC","A separate Part of the Constitution covers only SPSCs"],"correct_answer":"Article 315 covers both UPSC and SPSC","explanation":"Article 315 provides for both the Union and State PSCs. The same Part XIV (Articles 315-323) covers both UPSC and SPSCs. The same constitutional framework governs both with necessary adaptations.","chapter_number":49,"level":1},

    # CH49 L2 (+21)
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC and its advisory role:\n1. UPSC must be consulted on matters of promotions in central services involving change from one service to another.\n2. UPSC must be consulted on disciplinary matters affecting members of civil services.\n3. The Government must follow UPSC's advice in all matters where consultation is constitutionally mandated.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — UPSC's advice is advisory; the government can reject it but must explain in the annual report. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect mandatory consultation under Article 320(3).","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the independence of UPSC from political interference:\n1. UPSC members cannot be reappointed after their term — preventing them from trying to please the government to secure reappointment.\n2. UPSC members cannot return to government employment after retirement.\n3. The government can reduce UPSC members' salaries by ordinary legislation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — UPSC members' salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund (Article 322) and cannot be reduced to their disadvantage after appointment. Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe constitutional protections.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC consultation requirements:\n1. The Government is required to consult UPSC before making lateral entry appointments at Joint Secretary level.\n2. Temporary appointments of less than 1 year are exempt from UPSC consultation under the Constitution.\n3. Emergency appointments during national emergencies are completely exempt from UPSC consultation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 — lateral entry is now done with UPSC involvement but the constitution does not specifically require it for lateral entry per se. Statement 3 is incorrect — there is no blanket emergency exemption. Only Statement 2 is clearly constitutionally correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the State Public Service Commissions (SPSCs):\n1. A State PSC can be converted into a joint SPSC serving multiple states by an Act of Parliament.\n2. The Union can direct any SPSC to take over recruitment for a central service within that state.\n3. The UPSC can exercise jurisdiction over state recruitment if Parliament so authorises under Article 321.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — the Union cannot simply direct a SPSC to handle central recruitment; it requires specific legal authority. Statements 1 and 3 are correct under Articles 315 and 321 respectively.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Civil Services Examination reforms:\n1. The Kothari Commission (1974) introduced the three-stage format (Prelims+Mains+Interview).\n2. The Baswan Committee (2016) recommended reducing the interview marks weightage in CSE.\n3. The 2011 reform introduced the CSAT (aptitude test) as Paper-II of CSE Prelims.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Kothari Commission reformed the examination format. Baswan Committee (2016) recommended lowering interview marks. CSAT was introduced in 2011 as Paper-II of CSE Prelims.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC's organisational independence:\n1. UPSC sets its own rules of procedure under Article 318.\n2. The President can make regulations regarding UPSC's conditions of service.\n3. Parliament can, by law, restrict or expand UPSC's functions beyond what is stated in Article 320.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Article 318 allows UPSC to regulate its own functioning. The President makes service conditions regulations. Article 321 allows Parliament to expand UPSC's jurisdiction.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'All India Services' and UPSC:\n1. The IAS and IPS are All India Services serving both the Centre and the States.\n2. Officers of All India Services are recruited nationally by UPSC but allotted to State cadres.\n3. The Central Government alone can create new All India Services under the Constitution.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — under Article 312, new All India Services can be created by Parliament by law if the Rajya Sabha passes a resolution declaring it necessary in the national interest (not by the executive alone). Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the independence of SPSC Chairmen:\n1. A State PSC Chairman can be removed only on grounds proven before the Supreme Court — same as UPSC Chairman.\n2. A State PSC Chairman after retirement cannot serve under that state government.\n3. A State PSC Chairman is eligible to serve as UPSC Chairman after their state term.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. SPSC removal is through SC inquiry (same provision as UPSC). Post-retirement, the SPSC Chairman cannot serve the state but CAN be appointed as UPSC Chairman (a different body). All three are correct under Articles 316-317.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about reserved categories in UPSC examinations:\n1. Reservation of seats for SC/ST in UPSC examinations is constitutionally mandated.\n2. Age relaxations for SC/ST/OBC candidates in UPSC examinations are statutory provisions, not directly constitutional.\n3. The OBC reservation in central services was introduced after the Mandal Commission report implementation (1990).\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is overstated — reservations in recruitment are mandated by Article 16(4)/(4A) and implementing statutes, not a specific constitutional mandate to UPSC. Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC's role in disciplinary proceedings:\n1. UPSC must be consulted before a central civil servant is dismissed, removed, or reduced in rank.\n2. UPSC's advice in disciplinary cases is binding on the appointing authority.\n3. If a government employee appeals against a disciplinary order, UPSC must be consulted on the appeal too.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — UPSC's disciplinary advice is also advisory (not binding); government can differ but must record reasons. Statements 1 and 3 are correct — UPSC advice is required at both original and appeal stages.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Annual Reports of UPSC:\n1. The UPSC's Annual Report is presented to the President who causes it to be laid before Parliament.\n2. The Annual Report must specifically mention cases where UPSC advice was not accepted by the Government.\n3. UPSC's Annual Report contains data on examinations conducted, posts filled, and cases referred.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct about UPSC's Annual Report under Article 323. The President presents it to Parliament with a memorandum on cases of deviation from UPSC advice.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC and 'efficiency of service':\n1. UPSC advises on matters related to 'efficiency of public services.'\n2. UPSC has the power to initiate inquiries into departmental inefficiency suo motu.\n3. The government can seek UPSC's advice on general personnel policy reforms (not just individual appointments).\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — UPSC does not have suo motu powers to initiate efficiency inquiries. It is a consultative body, not an inspectorate. Statements 1 and 3 are correct under Article 320.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about constitutional provisions on UPSC:\n1. Article 315 — establishment of PSC; Article 316 — appointment and term of members; Article 317 — removal.\n2. Article 319 — ineligibility of members for further appointments under government; Article 320 — functions; Article 321 — extension of functions.\n3. Article 322 — expenses charged to CRF; Article 323 — annual report to President.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three correctly map the constitutional provisions from Article 315 to 323 covering all aspects of UPSC's constitutional framework. This is a correct and complete mapping.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC examinations and judicial review:\n1. UPSC's examination results can be challenged in court if there is evidence of procedural irregularity.\n2. Courts generally defer to UPSC's expertise in setting question standards and evaluation.\n3. A successful court challenge to UPSC examination can lead to invalidation of the entire examination.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. UPSC decisions are subject to judicial review for procedural violation (not merits of questions per se). Courts defer on academic matters. Serious irregularities have led to cancellation of entire examinations.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the comparison between UPSC and UK Civil Service Commission:\n1. Both were influenced by the Macaulay Committee recommendations on merit-based civil service recruitment.\n2. UK's Civil Service Commission is statutory while India's UPSC is constitutional.\n3. Both UPSC and UK Civil Service Commission conduct examinations in a similar three-stage format.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the UK's selection process differs significantly from India's three-stage examination format. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the historical and structural differences.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'service rules' framed by the President for central civil services:\n1. The President can frame rules regulating conditions of service of central civil service members under Article 309.\n2. Such rules must be preceded by UPSC consultation as mandated by Article 320(3)(b).\n3. Parliament can pass laws on conditions of service that override the President's service rules.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Article 309 allows the President to make service rules. Article 320 requires UPSC consultation. Parliament's laws override executive rules (proviso to Article 309).","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC and the concept of 'merit' in civil services:\n1. The Constitution explicitly defines 'merit' as the basis for civil service appointment.\n2. 'Merit' in practice includes performance in written examination AND interview scores.\n3. Reservation policies are a constitutionally recognised exception to purely merit-based appointment.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — the Constitution does not explicitly define 'merit'; Article 16(1) mandates equality of opportunity. 'Merit' as examination performance is a policy practice. Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'probationary period' for IAS recruits:\n1. IAS recruits undergo two years of probation including the Foundation Course (LBSNAA) and district training.\n2. During probation, the recruit can be discharged without formal disciplinary proceedings.\n3. UPSC must be consulted before discharging a probationer from IAS for poor performance.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — probationer discharge procedures differ from confirmed employee dismissal; UPSC consultation is not mandatory for routine probation discharge (specific rules apply). Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'cadre allocation' system for IAS/IPS:\n1. After UPSC selection, IAS/IPS officers are allotted to state cadres by the DoPT.\n2. Only home state allocation is given — officers are always posted to their home state cadre.\n3. The new cadre allocation policy (2017) allocates based on merit, home zone preference, and overall system needs.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — the system is NOT purely home state; it's a zone-based system. Toppers get preference for their home zone but not guaranteed home state. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Pension (Amendment) Act 2003 and civil services:\n1. The National Pension System (NPS) replaced the defined-benefit pension for central government recruits from January 1, 2004.\n2. UPSC selected officers recruited before January 1, 2004 continue under the old pension system.\n3. The NPS is a constitutionally mandated change for civil servants.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — NPS is a statutory/executive change, not a constitutional mandate. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the NPS transition.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the significance of UPSC being a constitutional body:\n1. Constitutional status makes UPSC's existence permanent — it cannot be abolished by ordinary legislation.\n2. Constitutional status insulates UPSC from executive control and interference.\n3. Constitutional status gives UPSC the power of a court — its decisions are final and binding.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — UPSC is consultative, not judicial; its decisions are advisory and subject to judicial review, not final and binding. Statements 1 and 2 correctly capture the significance of constitutional status.","chapter_number":49,"level":2},

    # CH49 L3 (+22)
    {"text":"Consider the following about the constitutional architecture of Article 315 and UPSC:\n1. The framers of the Constitution deliberately created UPSC as a constitutional body to insulate civil service recruitment from political patronage.\n2. The British Northcote-Trevelyan reforms (1854) and Macaulay Committee served as the intellectual basis for India's merit-based UPSC system.\n3. The creation of UPSC was a radical departure from the pre-independence system where the ICS was recruited purely through patronage.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the ICS was already recruited through merit-based examinations (since Macaulay reforms 1854); UPSC continued the merit system. The departure was from the patronage-based pre-Macaulay era. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC and federalism tensions:\n1. The creation of All India Services (IAS/IPS) was resisted by some states as an imposition on state autonomy.\n2. Sardar Patel argued for All India Services as necessary for national unity.\n3. The Sarkaria Commission (1988) recommended abolishing All India Services to strengthen state autonomy.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — Sarkaria Commission actually recommended strengthening All India Services, not abolishing them. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the historical debate.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Lateral Entry Scheme vs. UPSC-based recruitment:\n1. Proponents argue lateral entry brings domain expertise that generalist IAS officers lack.\n2. Critics argue lateral entry bypasses equal opportunity guarantees under Article 16.\n3. The Constitution explicitly prohibits lateral entry into IAS and IPS.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the Constitution does not prohibit lateral entry; it is a policy matter. The debate is about equality and efficiency. Statements 1 and 2 capture the genuine arguments.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about judicial oversight of UPSC decisions:\n1. The SC has held that UPSC's selection decisions are administrative in nature and fully subject to judicial review.\n2. Courts apply a 'light touch' standard of judicial review to UPSC examination matters — deferring to expert educational judgement.\n3. A UPSC candidate who was adversely affected by paper leaks has no remedy except through a fresh examination.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — candidates have remedies including writ petitions, and the court can direct fresh examinations or specific relief. Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the judicial review framework.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'reservation within reservation' debate in UPSC:\n1. The SC in Jarnail Singh (2018) held that the government may provide reservations for Scheduled Castes in promotion.\n2. The SC's Indra Sawhney judgment (1992) capped OBC reservation at 27% with no creamy layer exclusion requirement.\n3. The 103rd Constitutional Amendment (2019) introduced 10% EWS reservation in UPSC examinations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — Indra Sawhney DID introduce the creamy layer exclusion concept for OBCs. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the constitutional validity of the National Recruitment Agency (NRA):\n1. The NRA was created by executive order, without Parliamentary legislation or constitutional amendment.\n2. Critics argue the NRA reduces UPSC's domain, potentially undermining the constitutional scheme.\n3. The NRA handles recruitment for posts for which UPSC consultation was otherwise mandatory.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — NRA handles Group C/non-gazetted posts (SSC/IBPS/Railways scope) for which UPSC consultation was NOT mandatory in the first place. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'merit vs. representation' tension in civil services:\n1. The Constitution emphasises both merit (Article 16(1)) and representation (Article 16(4)) as simultaneous values.\n2. The SC's balancing approach in M. Nagaraj (2006) holds that reservations cannot be unlimited and must be based on data showing backwardness, inadequate representation, and overall administrative efficiency.\n3. The 'efficiency' criterion in Article 335 for SC/ST appointments has been used to justify limiting reservations at senior levels.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three correctly describe the constitutional tension between merit and representation: Articles 16(1) vs 16(4), Nagaraj's quantifiable data test, and Article 335's efficiency constraint. All three are accurate.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 2023 UPSC paper leak controversy and systemic issues:\n1. Paper leaks in UPSC examinations violate the fundamental right to equality of opportunity under Article 16(1).\n2. UPSC has sole constitutional responsibility to prevent paper leaks — courts can hold UPSC liable.\n3. The Central government overhauled UPSC's paper security protocols following the 2023 leak controversy.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is overstated — while UPSC has responsibility, paper leaks often occur through printing/logistical channels managed by agencies outside UPSC. Courts can direct systemic reforms. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about potential reforms for UPSC:\n1. Some scholars propose expanding the interview/personality test weight to better assess soft skills.\n2. Others argue that reducing the interview weight prevents subjective bias and casteism in selection.\n3. The Baswan Committee (2016) recommended reducing interview marks from 275 to 150.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — the reform debate runs the other way; scholars who cite bias argue FOR reducing interview weight, not expanding it. Statements 2 and 3 are correct about the Baswan Committee and the bias argument.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'constitutional morality' and UPSC's independence:\n1. B.R. Ambedkar stressed constitutional morality as essential for institutions like UPSC to function beyond political pressures.\n2. Constitutional morality requires that UPSC members resist political pressure even when the government formally disagrees with their recommendations.\n3. Constitutional morality has no specific bearing on UPSC — it applies only to courts.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — constitutional morality (Ambedkar's concept) applies to all constitutional institutions and citizens, not just courts. Statements 1 and 2 correctly extend constitutional morality to UPSC's functioning.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the evolution of civil service structure in India after independence:\n1. The Constitution specifically mandated the continuation of the Indian Civil Service (ICS) as the IAS.\n2. The ICS was replaced by the IAS through the Indian Administrative Service Act 1951.\n3. Former ICS officer D.C. Pavate objected to the IAS taking over ICS roles, creating the first civil services controversy.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — the Constitution did not mandate ICS continuation as such; Article 312 created the framework for All India Services. Statement 3 has no documented basis. Statement 2 is correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about civil services neutrality as a constitutional value:\n1. The Constitution expects civil servants to be politically neutral and serve governments of any party.\n2. Civil servants' neutrality is explicitly protected by Article 311 — which prevents arbitrary dismissal for political reasons.\n3. The 2nd Administrative Reforms Commission (2009) recommended mandatory political neutrality codes for senior civil servants.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Constitutional expectation of neutrality, Article 311's protection, and the 2nd ARC's recommendation on codes of conduct all reflect this value. All three are accurate.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the comparative performance of merit-based civil service systems:\n1. Studies show that countries with merit-based civil services (like India's UPSC model) tend to have better governance outcomes than patronage-based systems.\n2. India's civil service, despite merit recruitment, has faced criticism for poor implementation, corruption, and red-tapism.\n3. The persistence of governance failures despite merit recruitment suggests that selection quality alone is insufficient without accountability systems.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct and reflect the scholarly consensus on civil service reform: merit matters but is insufficient without complementary accountability and incentive structures.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'UPSC paparazzi culture' and coaching industry:\n1. The massive private coaching industry for UPSC creates significant inequity — coaching provides advantage to wealthy urban candidates.\n2. This inequity arguably violates the spirit of Article 16(1) — equal opportunity in public employment.\n3. The Constitution mandates the government to provide free civil service coaching to all aspirants.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — there is no constitutional mandate for free coaching; this is a policy choice. Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the equity concern with the coaching-dependent system.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about civil service accountability mechanisms:\n1. The Central Vigilance Commission (CVC) oversees anti-corruption measures for Group A civil servants.\n2. The Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) cannot investigate an IAS officer without the prior permission of the state government if the officer is serving in a state cadre.\n3. The Lokpal Act 2013 includes Group A civil servants within Lokpal's jurisdiction.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. CVC's role, DSPE Act's prior sanction requirement (single directive modified after SC ruling), and Lokpal's jurisdiction over Group A officers (Lokpal Act) — all three are accurate.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 2013 Supreme Court ruling on 'transfers' and civil service independence:\n1. The SC in T.S.R. Subramanian v. Union of India (2013) held that civil servants cannot be transferred as punishment without following due process.\n2. The SC recommended that civil servants should not be required to follow unlawful oral orders of ministers.\n3. Parliament responded to this ruling by enacting the Civil Services Standards, Performance, and Accountability Act.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — no such dedicated act has been enacted by Parliament; the ruling's implementation has been sporadic through administrative guidelines. Statements 1 and 2 correctly capture the T.S.R. Subramanian ruling.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the psychological and sociological impact of UPSC on Indian society:\n1. UPSC aspirants constitute millions of young Indians in a state of prolonged career uncertainty — the 'UPSC aspirant' has become a sociological phenomenon.\n2. The UPSC system has been credited with building a unified, geographically mobile 'steel frame' of governance across India's diversity.\n3. The vast gap between supply of UPSC aspirants and available seats is itself a constitutional problem requiring governmental remedy.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the mismatch between aspirants and seats is a social and policy concern, not in itself a constitutional violation requiring a constitutional remedy. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the concept of 'civil service reform' and the 2nd Administrative Reforms Commission (2005-2009):\n1. The 2nd ARC recommended a Civil Services Act to give legal framework for civil service functioning, replacing the existing executive-rule based system.\n2. The 2nd ARC recommended fixed minimum tenures for civil servants to prevent arbitrary transfers.\n3. All 2nd ARC recommendations on civil services reform have been implemented by the government.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — many 2nd ARC recommendations remain unimplemented, including the Civil Services Act. Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the ARC's key recommendations.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the UPSC system and digital governance challenges:\n1. The 2024 UPSC Main examination controversies highlighted issues of AI-generated answers and digital integrity.\n2. UPSC has experimented with computer-based testing for preliminary stages.\n3. The Constitution must be amended to allow for digital/online UPSC examinations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — no constitutional amendment is required; UPSC can adopt digital testing under its existing powers. Statements 1 and 2 reflect current developments.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about UPSC and government deviations from UPSC advice:\n1. The Annual Report mechanism (Article 323) creates parliamentary accountability when the government overrides UPSC advice.\n2. The frequency of government deviations from UPSC advice indicates politicisation of the recruitment process.\n3. The SC can compel the government to follow UPSC advice in individual appointment cases.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — courts do not compel the government to follow advisory opinions; they can quash procedurally flawed decisions but not substitute UPSC's advisory opinion as binding. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the comparative constitutional independence of UPSC vs. judiciary:\n1. Both UPSC members and SC judges cannot be removed by the executive acting alone.\n2. Both UPSC members and SC judges have salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund.\n3. Both UPSC and the SC are fully insulated from all forms of executive pressure — the constitutional protections are equivalent.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the protections differ. SC judges have stronger protection (collegium, writ jurisdiction). UPSC advice is advisory; it operates in a recommendatory capacity unlike the binding constitutional role of courts. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'administrative generalist vs. specialist' debate in Indian civil services:\n1. The IAS system trains generalist administrators who move across domains, which some argue reduces departmental expertise.\n2. Several committees (Hota Committee, ARC-II) have recommended domain-specific streams within the IAS.\n3. The Constitution mandates the IAS to be a generalist service — domain specialisation would require constitutional amendment.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — there is no constitutional requirement for the IAS to be 'generalist.' Domain specialisation is a policy matter. Statements 1 and 2 are correct about the reform debate.","chapter_number":49,"level":3},
]
